BATCH={'key':'ia_davenport_nddn','label':'Davenport, Iowa','added':'2026-11-23 09:35'}
PLACES=[
N('first-national-bank-davenport','First National Bank Building','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52082,-90.57574,'exact','view','walk','','1924–',
  'First National Bank of Davenport was the first bank in the country to open under the National Banking and Currency Act of 1863. This nine-storey building of 1924 is the third the bank put up on this corner, replacing one lost to fire; it was designed by O. H. Breidert of the Chicago firm Childs & Smith in the Renaissance Revival style. After the bank failed in the 1933 Bank Holiday the upper floors stayed let to offices while the banking floor was used for shops; it is now known as the US Bank Building.',
  'A stone-faced front with a tall arcade of round arches whose capitals are carved with eagles and buffalo. Over the Second Street door is a bronze screen with four scenes of mining, coining, counting and trading money, two reclining figures by Adolph A. Weinman above the arch, and carved figures by Corrado Parducci inside it. An inscription above the third floor says this was the first national bank in the United States; a clock from the Scott County Savings Bank is on the north-east corner.',
  'The first national bank in the country, and it says so on the wall.',
  'Office building at 201 West Second Street, downtown, with a bank as its main tenant. Seen from the street; whether visitors may look into the banking hall was not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('First National Bank Building (Davenport, Iowa)')],
  brick='no'),
N('davenport-bank-and-trust-building','Davenport Bank and Trust Building','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52232,-90.57578,'exact','view','walk','','1926–1927',
  'Built in 1926 and 1927 at Third and Main streets for the bank that became Davenport Bank and Trust, for much of the 20th century the leading bank of the Quad Cities and at one time Iowa\'s largest commercial bank. The Chicago firm of Weary & Alford designed it, and it is still the tallest building in the Quad Cities. The bank was sold to Norwest in 1993 and became Wells Fargo, which left in 2016; the upper floors were partly turned into apartments from 2013, and the building is now called Davenport Bank Apartments.',
  'A twelve-storey limestone tower (buff brick on most of the south side) topped by a pedimented temple and a three-storey clock tower with an eagle at each corner. Inside, the original banking room keeps its painted groin-vaulted ceiling, iron teller cages, and a 1928 painting by Hiram Thompsen of the treaty signed after the Black Hawk War; whether the public may go in now was not checked.',
  'The tallest building in the Quad Cities, with eagles round its clock.',
  'Privately owned apartment and commercial building at Third and Main streets, downtown. Seen from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Davenport Bank and Trust')],
  brick='no'),
N('kahl-building-capitol-theatre','Kahl Building and Capitol Theatre','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52272,-90.57810,'exact','open','walk','','1920–',
  'A ten-storey office block built in 1920 by Henry Kahl, designed by the Davenport architect Arthur Ebeling after the Chicago School and Louis Sullivan\'s Wainwright Building in St. Louis. Inside it the Capitol Theatre opened on Christmas Day 1920, a movie palace with an interior by Rapp & Rapp; it joined the RKO circuit in 1927, stopped showing films in 1977, and hosted the 1959 Winter Dance Party with Buddy Holly days before his death. The building was given to the Scott Community College Foundation in 1994, sold in 2018 and turned into apartments by 2023, and the theatre reopened in July 2023 after a restoration.',
  'Stone and terra cotta fronts, richly ornamented on the lower floors, plain above, with round-arched windows and a big cornice at the top. The theatre, seating about 2,000, keeps a Wicks theatre organ in its original home; the lobby\'s pink Alabama marble and the gold elevators were kept in the renovation.',
  'Buddy Holly played here five days before the crash.',
  'Privately owned, 326 West Third Street, downtown: apartments above, the Capitol Theatre below. The theatre is open for shows; its own page could not be read, so show and box office times were not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Kahl Building')],
  brick='no', hours='?'),
N('davenport-city-hall','Davenport City Hall','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52370,-90.57667,'exact','open','walk','','1895–',
  'Built in 1895, in the middle of a deep depression, without borrowing: most of the money came from a new state tax on the city\'s illegal saloons, not, as the local story has it, from fines on its brothels. The Davenport architect John W. Ross designed it in Richardsonian Romanesque. It replaced the 1857 city hall on Brady Street and is still the seat of the city government.',
  'Four storeys of Ohio Berea sandstone over a steel frame, with corner towers, gable ends, rough stone and round Roman arches, under a hipped roof. A 1963 addition is on the north side.',
  'A city hall paid for by a saloon tax.',
  'City of Davenport offices, 226 West Fourth Street, at Harrison Street, downtown. A public building; office hours were not found on the city\'s home page. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Davenport City Hall'),L('City of Davenport','https://www.davenportiowa.com/')],
  brick='no', hours='?'),
N('first-presbyterian-church-davenport-ia','First Presbyterian Church','Davenport','ia','worship','standing',41.53631,-90.57019,'exact','open','walk','','1898–1899',
  'The congregation formed in 1838 and was organised as a church in 1839 in a frame schoolhouse at Fourth and Harrison streets. After earlier churches downtown it built this one at Kirkwood Boulevard and Iowa Street, begun in March 1898 and dedicated in December 1899, to designs by Gottschalk & Beadle of Galesburg, Illinois, who built an almost identical church there. By 1935 it had about 1,300 members.',
  'A Romanesque church of Marquette brownstone with a Richardsonian tower and carved oak leaves on the gables. Inside, an octagonal room under a ceiling marked with Maltese and Greek crosses, stained glass by J&R Lamb Studios of New York, and a four-manual Casavant organ of 1999. Anyone may come to Sunday worship.',
  '',
  'Active Presbyterian (PC USA) congregation, 1702 Iowa Street. Seen from the street at any time; go in for Sunday worship. Times from the church\'s own page; office open weekdays. Parking not checked.',
  [W('First Presbyterian Church (Davenport, Iowa)'),L('First Presbyterian Church','https://www.fpcdavenport.org/')],
  brick='no', hours='Sunday worship 9:30 a.m.'),
N('st-johns-methodist-church-davenport','St. John\'s United Methodist Church','Davenport','ia','worship','standing',41.53366,-90.57387,'exact','open','walk','','1902–1903',
  'Methodists bought this hilltop corner at 14th and Brady streets in 1867, partly to get away from the noise of the trains on Fifth Street. The cornerstone of the present church was laid in 1902 and it was dedicated in December 1903, designed by Parke Burrows with Frederick G. Clausen as supervising architect.',
  'A cruciform English Gothic church of limestone with a big corner tower; the main door is under the tower\'s arch, and large tracery windows fill the west, north and south ends. The parsonage beside it is of the same stone. Anyone may come to the Sunday services.',
  '',
  'Active United Methodist congregation, 109 East 14th Street (at Brady Street). Seen from the street at any time; go in for Sunday worship. Times from the church\'s own page. Parking not checked.',
  [W('St. John\'s United Methodist Church (Davenport, Iowa)'),L('St. John\'s United Methodist Church','https://www.stjohnsumcdav.org/')],
  brick='no', hours='Sunday 9 a.m. (classic) and 11 a.m. (contemporary) in the sanctuary; 5:30 p.m. in the fellowship hall'),
N('hotel-blackhawk-davenport','Hotel Blackhawk','Davenport','ia','hotel','standing',41.52293,-90.57192,'exact','open','walk','','1915–',
  'The first seven floors of the "New Fireproof Hotel Blackhawk" opened in February 1915 on the site of the Saratoga Hotel, built for about a million dollars by W. F. Miller; four more floors in 1920 brought it to 400 rooms. Herbert Hoover, Richard Nixon, Carl Sandburg and Jack Dempsey stayed here, the big bands played its ballroom, and Cary Grant was staying here when he died in Davenport. It went bankrupt in the 1970s and stood empty until a renovation in 1978 and 1979; it is now a working hotel joined to the RiverCenter convention centre.',
  'An eleven-storey brick and terra cotta hotel. The lobby and the ballrooms (the Gold Room among them) are inside; the interior was not checked beyond that.',
  'The hotel where Cary Grant died.',
  'A working hotel, 200 East Third Street, downtown. Its lobby is open as a hotel\'s is; the hotel\'s café gives its own hours on the hotel\'s page. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Hotel Blackhawk'),L('Hotel Blackhawk','https://www.hotelblackhawk.com/')],
  brick='?', hours='?'),
N('hotel-mississippi-adler-theatre','Hotel Mississippi and Adler Theatre (RKO Orpheum)','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52261,-90.57350,'exact','open','walk','','1931–',
  'The last of Davenport\'s big downtown hotels, opened in November 1931 with 200 rooms and 50 apartments, wrapped round a seven-storey movie palace, the RKO Orpheum; A. S. Graven was the architect and Henry Dreyfuss worked on it. By the early 1980s the hotel had become apartments. The theatre, renamed the Adler, is home to the Quad City Symphony Orchestra and Ballet Quad Cities; the hotel part was renovated into 56 apartments by 2007, with its lobby restored.',
  'A ten-storey L-shaped Art Deco block of brick and granite around the theatre. The theatre opens for performances; its lobby detail is Art Deco.',
  '',
  'The Adler Theatre, 136 East Third Street, downtown, open for its performances (dates and doors on its own page); the Mississippi Lofts above are private apartments. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Mississippi Lofts and Adler Theatre'),L('Adler Theatre','https://www.adlertheatre.com/')],
  brick='?', hours='?'),
N('ambrose-hall-davenport','Ambrose Hall, St. Ambrose University','Davenport','ia','inst','standing',41.53876,-90.58127,'exact','view','walk','','1885–1912',
  'The first building on the campus of St. Ambrose, founded in 1882 by the first Catholic bishop of Davenport and first taught in two classrooms at St. Margaret\'s Cathedral. Bishop Henry Cosgrove bought Noel\'s Grove on Locust Street, the cornerstone was laid on July 5, 1885, and the central section with its tower and spire went up first, designed by Victor Huot. Wings were added in five phases to 1912, and the building held the whole college until 1927.',
  'A Second Empire building of yellow stone and red brick with a central pavilion under a mansard tower and spire, round-arched windows with stone hoods, and trefoil and hexafoil windows marking the old third-floor chapel. The mansard roof was plainly rebuilt in the 1960s; the tower, spire and bell were restored in a renovation reported in 2013.',
  '',
  'St. Ambrose University, 518 West Locust Street. Seen from the campus walks and the street; inside is university space (not checked). Parking not checked.',
  [W('Ambrose Hall')],
  brick='yes'),
N('trinity-episcopal-cathedral-davenport','Trinity Episcopal Cathedral','Davenport','ia','worship','standing',41.53134,-90.57495,'exact','open','walk','','1867–1873; spire 1998',
  'Built as Grace Cathedral on the old Griswold College grounds, to designs by Edward Tuckerman Potter of New York: the cornerstone was laid in 1867 and it was consecrated in June 1873, one of the oldest cathedrals of the Episcopal Church in the country. Trinity Church, the town\'s first Episcopal parish, merged with it and the cathedral was renamed in 1910; it is kept as the historical cathedral of the Diocese of Iowa. Its bell tower and spire waited 131 years, until a gift in 1998 paid for them.',
  'Native Iowa limestone trimmed with Indiana limestone; buttressed bays of triple lancets, a rose window (in memory of Bishop Kemper) over the main door, iron ornament along the roof ridge, and 19th-century stained glass, including nine lancets in the apse. The spire is metal-clad, not the stone one first planned. Anyone may come to the services.',
  'A cathedral that got its spire 131 years late.',
  'Active Episcopal cathedral, 121 West 12th Street, on the bluff above downtown. Seen from the street at any time; go in for services. Times from the cathedral\'s own page; for weekday morning prayer the page says to use the 12th Street entrance. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Trinity Episcopal Cathedral (Davenport, Iowa)'),L('Trinity Episcopal Cathedral','https://www.qctrinity.org/')],
  brick='no', hours='Sunday Eucharist 10 a.m.; Tuesday Eucharist 11 a.m.; morning prayer Monday to Thursday 8:30 a.m.'),
N('sacred-heart-cathedral-davenport','Sacred Heart Cathedral','Davenport','ia','worship','standing',41.53006,-90.56929,'exact','open','walk','','1889–1891',
  'The parish began in 1856 as St. Margaret\'s on land given by Antoine and Marguerite LeClaire; St. Margaret\'s, which served as the cathedral, was set on fire by an arsonist in 1873. The present church, designed by the Chicago architect James J. Egan with Victor Huot supervising, was begun in 1889 and dedicated on November 15, 1891, and took the name Sacred Heart. It stands on Cork Hill, the old Irish quarter. The convent beside it was torn down in 2012 and the school in 2017.',
  'A Gothic Revival basilica with a corner tower and spire, brick walls clad in rock-faced Bedford stone on an Anamosa limestone foundation. Brightly coloured stained glass (the Twelve Apostles along the nave, a rose window, a great window over the altar), cleaned and repaired in 2019, and a Noack pipe organ. The 1895 limestone rectory is next door. Anyone may come to Mass.',
  '',
  'Active Catholic cathedral of the Diocese of Davenport, 422 East 10th Street. Seen from the street at any time; go in for Mass. Times from the parish\'s own page; the parish office is open weekdays. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Sacred Heart Cathedral (Davenport, Iowa)'),L('Sacred Heart Cathedral','https://shcdavenport.org/')],
  brick='no', hours='Mass Saturday 4 p.m.; Sunday 7:15, 9 and 11 a.m. (Vietnamese); Monday, Wednesday, Friday 5:30 p.m.; Tuesday, Thursday 8 a.m.'),
N('central-fire-station-davenport','Central Fire Station','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52304,-90.57943,'exact','view','walk','','1901–1902',
  'Built in 1901 and 1902 to designs by the Davenport architect Gustav Hanssen, when the city wanted a larger downtown station than its small hose houses; the paid fire department itself dated from 1882. It is described as the oldest working fire station west of the Mississippi and is still the department\'s headquarters. A 1940s addition was pulled down and replaced by a modern one in 2016.',
  'An Italianate and Neoclassical front on Scott Street with a pedimented entrance between two towers; the hose tower, still used to dry hoses, once had a taller bell loft, cut down before 1953. The 2016 building stands beside the old one.',
  '',
  'Working fire station of the Davenport Fire Department, 331 Scott Street, downtown. Seen from the street; do not block the doors. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Central Fire Station (Davenport, Iowa)')],
  brick='?'),
N('col-ballroom-davenport','The Col Ballroom (Saengerfest Halle, the Coliseum)','Davenport','ia','amuse','standing',41.52360,-90.58829,'exact','view','walk','','1914–',
  'Davenport hosted the great German singing festival, the Saengerfest, in 1898, and built a wooden 4,000-seat hall for it; it became a dance hall called the Coliseum and burned in October 1913. This brick hall across West Fourth Street replaced it in November 1914, designed by Clausen & Burrows. As The Col it hosted Duke Ellington, B.B. King, Johnny Cash, Chuck Berry and Jimi Hendrix, among many; it closed as a ballroom and concert venue on October 29, 2018.',
  'A steel-frame brick hall with an arched roof, two-storey corner pavilions, and tiles across the top of the front each carrying one letter of "Coliseum"; a heart-shaped sign reading "The Col Ballroom" hangs over the door. Performers signed a back wall. Its use since 2018 was not checked.',
  'A dance hall for a German song festival that Hendrix later played.',
  'Privately owned, 1012 West Fourth Street, in the West End. Closed as a venue in 2018: seen from the street only. Parking not checked.',
  [W('The Col Ballroom')],
  brick='?'),
N('old-city-hall-davenport','Old City Hall (Oxford Flats)','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52508,-90.57435,'exact','view','walk','','1857–',
  'Built in 1857 as an engine house for a fire company, to designs by Willett Carroll; in 1858 its hall became the council chamber, and it served as city hall and fire station together until 1877, and as city hall until the present one was built in 1895. The city sold it in 1900, and about 1910 it was turned into six flats and rebuilt to its present look as Oxford Flats. It is the oldest building in Davenport tied to its city government.',
  'A three-storey brick building on a stone foundation, now Neoclassical, with a two-storey polygonal oriel window decorated with fleurs-de-lis. The 1857 tower and Italian Renaissance front are gone.',
  '',
  'Private apartments, 514 Brady Street, just north of downtown. Seen from the street only. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Old City Hall (Davenport, Iowa)')],
  brick='?',apart='davenport-city-hall: the 1895 city hall on West Fourth Street; this is the 1857 engine house that served before it'),
N('central-high-school-davenport','Central High School (Davenport High School)','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.53000,-90.57578,'site','view','walk','','1905–1907',
  'Opened in January 1907 as Davenport High School on the old Griswold College grounds, which the school board bought from the Episcopal Diocese of Iowa for $53,000. Clausen & Burrows designed it; building began in 1905 and it cost $347,000, with room for 1,600 pupils, a 1,300-seat auditorium and a gymnasium. It is still one of the city\'s three public high schools and stands in the College Square Historic District.',
  'Four storeys (three above a tall basement) faced in Marquette "rain-drop" sandstone under a red tile roof; the original floors were terrazzo mosaic and maple.',
  '',
  'A working public high school of the Davenport Community School District, 1120 Main Street. Seen from the street; not open to visitors. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Central High School (Davenport, Iowa)')],
  brick='no'),
N('parker-ficke-house-davenport','J. Monroe Parker–Ficke House','Davenport','ia','landmark','standing',41.53210,-90.57593,'exact','view','walk','','1881–',
  'Built in 1881 for James Monroe Parker, a wealthy Davenport financier, to designs by T. W. McClelland and Victor Huot. It later belonged to C. A. Ficke, mayor of Davenport in 1890 and 1891 and the first to veto an ordinance passed by the council. It stands in the College Square Historic District.',
  'A two-storey Second Empire brick house on an irregular plan with a mansard roof of multicoloured slate, iron cresting along the roof, brackets and fanciful window surrounds.',
  '',
  'Private, 1208 Main Street. Seen from the street only. Parking not checked.',
  [W('J. Monroe Parker–Ficke House')],
  brick='?'),
N('burtis-opera-house-davenport','Burtis Opera House (Cusack building)','Davenport','ia','civic','standing',41.52394,-90.57224,'exact','view','walk','','1867–; rebuilt after 1921',
  'The Burtis Opera House opened in 1867 at 415 Perry Street and seated 1,600; Mark Twain lectured here in 1869, Susan B. Anthony spoke for women\'s votes in 1874, and the Tri-City Symphony gave its first concert here in 1916. An arsonist burned it on April 26, 1921. Its owner had the shell rebuilt as an office for the Thomas Cusack Company, a Chicago billboard firm, losing the second and third storeys and the mansard roof. The Burtis-Kimball House hotel next door, where Ronald Reagan once lived, was torn down in 1993.',
  'A one-storey remnant in a Renaissance Revival dress, with a flat roof, a raised parapet over the entrance and altered arched windows from the old opera house front; it stands alone beside the raised railroad tracks.',
  'All that is left of the stage where Mark Twain spoke.',
  'Privately owned or leased office building at 415 Perry Street, downtown (its present user was not confirmed). Seen from the street only. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Burtis–Kimball House Hotel/Burtis Opera House')],
  brick='?'),
]
